{
  "title": "Week 2: Arrays and Strings - Instructor Draft",
  "body": "## Project Directions: String Array Manipulation and Analysis  (Difficulty: Intermediate)  Students will write a C program that demonstrates the relationship between strings and character arrays, specifically focusing on memory layout, ASCII representation, and string termination.  The program must handle user input via command-line arguments and process the input as an array of characters.  Students must calculate and display the length of the string (excluding the NUL terminator) and the sum of the ASCII values of all characters in the string.  The program should also demonstrate how to access individual characters using array indexing and how the string terminates with a `NUL` character (`'0'`).  Students must compile their code using a standard C compiler (e.g., `clang` or `gcc`) and ensure it runs without segmentation faults or memory errors.  The final submission must include the source code, a terminal log showing the compilation process, and a terminal log showing the program execution with at least two different command-line arguments of varying lengths.",
  "citations": [1, 2, 5, 6, 7, 8]
}

{
  "title": "Course FAQs: Arrays and Strings",
  "body": "Q: What is a string in the context of this course? A: A string is simply an array of values of type `char`: an array of characters [6]. It begins with the first character and ends with a special character called a `NUL character` (the `0` character) [1]. Q: How are strings stored in memory? A: Strings are stored as arrays of characters, which means they are stored back-to-back in memory [1]. Similarly, arrays in general are a sequence of values that are stored back-to-back in memory [3]. Q: How can I access individual characters within a string? A: You can treat a string as an array and access individual characters using index notation, such as `s[0]`, `s[1]`, etc. [8]. Q: What is the difference between `NULL` and `NUL`? A: `NUL` (with one L) refers to the `0` character used to end a string, whereas `NULL` (with two L's) is a different concept [1]. Q: How do I declare an array of strings? A: You can declare an array of strings by specifying the type `string` followed by the array name and size in brackets, for example: `string words[2];` [7]. Q: How are command-line arguments passed to programs? A: The course material mentions that command-line arguments can be passed to programs as part of the lesson on arrays [2], though specific syntax for accessing them is not detailed in the provided text. Q: What is the role of a compiler? A: A compiler is a specialized computer program that converts source code into machine code that can be understood by a computer [5].",
  "citations": [1, 1, 3, 6, 1, 2, 7, 5]
}

