{
  "title": "Pharmacokinetics I & II: Project Directions and Learning Outcomes",
  "body": "This project requires students to analyze the relationship between pharmacokinetic parameters and plasma drug concentrations. Students must explain how dose, bioavailability, rate of absorption, apparent volume of distribution, total clearance, and elimination half-life affect plasma concentrations following a single dose. The project involves calculating the apparent volume of distribution by extrapolating plasma concentration data to time zero and determining total clearance based on the mass of drug eliminated per unit time. Additionally, students will investigate the effect of infusions or multiple dosing on the time-course of plasma concentrations, specifically focusing on steady-state conditions where the plasma concentration is directly proportional to the input rate and inversely proportional to total plasma clearance. Students must also determine renal clearance by measuring the amount of drug excreted in urine over a specific time interval and interpolating plasma concentration at the midpoint of that interval.",
  "citations": [2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]
}

{
  "title": "Instructor Draft: Pharmacokinetic Principles and Calculations",
  "body": "This module covers the fundamental parameters of pharmacokinetics, focusing on how drug dose, bioavailability, and elimination kinetics influence plasma concentrations. Students will learn to calculate the apparent volume of distribution (Vd) by extrapolating plasma concentration data to time zero, where Vd equals the total dose divided by the initial concentration (C0). The concept of total clearance (ClT) is defined as the volume of plasma completely cleared of drug per unit time, which can be determined by dividing the mass of drug eliminated per unit time by the plasma concentration. The relationship between these parameters is critical for understanding steady-state conditions; specifically, the plasma concentration at steady-state (Css) is directly proportional to the input rate of the drug and inversely proportional to its total plasma clearance. Furthermore, the elimination half-life is directly proportional to Vd and inversely proportional to total clearance, meaning that for a given clearance, a higher volume of distribution results in a longer half-life. Students must also be able to explain how the area under the plasma concentration vs. time curve (AUC) depends on the dose, the fraction of dose absorbed (bioavailability), and total clearance, noting that bioavailability is experimentally determined by comparing the AUC of a specific dosage form to that of an intravenous dose under conditions of complete absorption.",
  "citations": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]
}

{
  "title": "Course FAQs: Pharmacokinetics Fundamentals",
  "body": "What is the definition of clearance in pharmacokinetics? Clearance is defined as the volume of plasma completely cleared of a drug per unit time by all routes and mechanisms (Passage 1). Specifically, renal clearance (ClR) is the volume of plasma completely cleared of drug per unit time (ml/min), calculated by dividing the excretion rate in urine by the plasma concentration at the midpoint of the time interval (Passage 4). How do pharmacokinetic parameters affect plasma concentrations? The plasma concentrations of a drug after a single dose are affected by the dose, bioavailability, rate of absorption, apparent volume of distribution, total clearance, and elimination half-life (Passage 2). What is the relationship between half-life, volume of distribution, and clearance? The plasma half-life is directly proportional to the volume of distribution (Vd) and inversely proportional to total clearance (ClT). For a given clearance, a higher Vd results in a longer half-life (Passage 6). How is the steady-state plasma concentration determined? At steady-state, the plasma concentration (Css) is directly proportional to the input rate (DR) of the drug and inversely proportional to its total plasma clearance (ClT). The relationship is expressed as Css = DR / ClT (Passage 7). What factors influence the area under the plasma concentration vs. time curve (AUC)? The AUC is dependent on the dose (D), the fraction of the dose absorbed (F), and the total clearance (ClT), calculated as AUC = (F * D) / ClT (Passage 8). How is bioavailability determined? Bioavailability (F) is the fraction of the dose absorbed into the systemic circulation. It is determined experimentally by measuring the AUC of a dosage form given by one route and comparing it to the AUC of the same dose of drug given intravenously (i.v.), which represents complete absorption (Passage 8).",
  "citations": [1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8, 8]
}
